# Glossary

Eight terms the rest of the imini learning material assumes. Plain language, with "where to see it."

A quick note on the pointers in parentheses: a name ending in `.java` is a source file under
`src/main/java/com/example/imini/`; a name ending in `.md` is a document (at the repo root, or in
`docs/` when written that way); a name ending in `.sh` or `.bat` is a script at the repo root. So
"see `AgentEngine.java`" means open that source file, and "see `docs/TRACE_EDIT.md`" means open that
document. A fuller index of the code each term points to is at the end.

**Harness.** Everything around the model: the loop, the tools, permissions, sessions, memory, and the
web/CLI surface. The model only produces text and tool requests; the *harness* decides what actually
happens. imini is a harness you can read. *(For the big picture, read `ARCHITECTURE.md`; the code starts
in `AgentLoop.java`, which hands the loop to `AgentEngine.java`.)*

**Agent loop.** The think → act → observe cycle. The model replies with either a final answer or a
**tool call**; the harness runs the tool, feeds the result back, and asks the model again — until it has a
final answer or a guard stops it. *(The loop lives in `AgentEngine.java`, in its `converse` method; you
can watch it run on a real task in `docs/TRACE_EDIT.md`.)*

**Tool call.** A structured request from the model to do something — read a file, search the repo, run a
command. The harness *validates* it against a schema, runs it, and returns the result as data. Tools are
how the model affects the world; it can't touch anything the harness doesn't expose. *(The list of tools
is assembled in `ToolRegistry.java`; each one is implemented in `BuiltinTools.java`.)*

**Plan mode.** A mode where the harness asks the model to *propose* steps without executing any
file-changing actions. You get a plan to review instead of changes to undo. *(The modes are defined in
`PermissionService.java`; start a run in plan mode with `plan.sh` or `plan.bat`.)*

**Checkpoint.** A saved snapshot taken before a file is changed, so an edit can be reversed (`rewind`).
A safe coding harness needs an undo story. *(Snapshots are taken and restored in `CheckpointStore.java`;
undo a change with `rewind.sh` or `rewind.bat`.)*

**Project memory.** Always-on instructions for a project, kept in an `IMINI.md` file and injected into the
system prompt every turn (e.g. "prefer concise prose"). Different from a one-off prompt or a slash command.
*(Loaded by `ProjectContext.java`; to switch it on, copy the template `IMINI.example.md` to `IMINI.md` and
edit it.)*

**MCP (Model Context Protocol).** A standard way to plug in *external* tool providers. The harness loads
them, exposes their tools, and fences their output — the model doesn't own them. *(Handled in
`McpManager.java`, which reads an `mcp.json` you create at the repo root.)*

**Prompt-injection fencing.** Treating tool/web/MCP output as *data, not instructions*. A fetched web page
might say "ignore your rules"; the harness wraps such output and reminds the model not to obey it.
*(The wrapping logic is in `Untrusted.java`; Module 11 of `docs/LEARNING_PATH.md` walks through it.)*

Once these eight make sense, the architecture and the learning-path modules will read easily.

